The user is asking about two statements, seemingly wants clarification, and says both "Must be added in next phase." They may be asking what practical implication is, and telling us to add these items in next phase (maybe phase 2 or later). Need explain in practice:

1. Wrapper output memory copied before next wasm call: In wasm, exported function returns pointer into wasm linear memory, not a JS string. `pathland_wasm_render` writes HTML into a buffer owned by Rust/wasm. The pointer remains valid only until the next render (or any call that mutates/reallocs the buffer). So in Node wrapper, immediately after call, create Uint8Array from memory.buffer at ptr/out_len, decode/copy to a JS string, and (ideally) copy bytes. Don't hold a view across calls; if wasm memory grows, the old ArrayBuffer detaches. Practical pattern:

```ts
const render = (batch, root) => {
  const ptr = alloc(batch.length);
  new Uint8Array(memory.buffer, ptr, batch.length).set(batch);
  const outLenPtr = alloc(4);
  const outPtr = wasm.render(ptr, batch.length, root, 0, outLenPtr);
  const len = new DataView(memory.buffer).getUint32(outLenPtr, true);
  // Copy immediately:
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(memory.buffer, outPtr, len).slice();
  free(ptr, batch.length); free(outLenPtr, 4);
  return new TextDecoder().decode(bytes);
};
```
Actually `TextDecoder.decode(new Uint8Array(...))` copies into string, so could decode immediately; but if you retain a view, don't. The returned pointer is not owned by JS. It's overwritten on next render. In practice this is a usage rule, not an extra feature; add a code comment/test/guard. Maybe "Must be added in next phase" means add this to next phase plan? We should acknowledge.

2. Conditional rendering: structural/conditional rendering = app can choose a different subtree at runtime based on signals (e.g. `if (loggedIn()) VStack(...) else Text(...)`), and engine diffs/retains tree, emitting TREE CREATE/DELETE/INSERT/MOVE deltas rather than re-emitting full tree. Phase 1 static tree only: body evaluated once, children functions evaluated once at mount; no structural reconciliation. In practice, views cannot change structure based on state in this phase; only text/property values can react. Adding it next phase means: engine monitors selector signals, re-evaluates slot content, diffs old/new subtree, preserves ids where possible, emits TREE deltas, registers/destroys bindings and input actions.

