In TypeScript, a @view decorator wraps a plain class definition and transforms it into a callable factory function at runtime.
Instead of requiring developers to manually write new MyView(props) or extend a base class, the decorator intercepts the class constructor and wraps it in a function that manages the component's lifecycle and return type.
How it Works Under the Hood
When TypeScript compiles a decorated class, it passes the target class to the decorator function. The decorator returns a new function (a factory) that:
Instantiates the class behind the scenes when invoked (e.g., calling MyTextView({ text: 'Hello' })).
Injects the props directly into the instance or its body(props) method.
Attaches Pathland DSL helpers (like .modifiers(...) and .children(...)) onto the returned view object.
Minimal Implementation Example
TypeScript
export interface ViewInstance {
  body(props: any): any;
}

// TC39 / TS 5.0+ Class Decorator
export function view<P, T extends { new (): ViewInstance }>(
  Target: T,
  context: ClassDecoratorContext
) {
  // Return a factory function instead of the raw class constructor
  const factory = function (props: P = {} as P) {
    const instance = new Target();

    return {
      props,
      // Evaluates body() when Pathland mounts the view tree
      renderNode() {
        return instance.body(props);
      },
      // Chainable modifier operation
      modifiers(...mods: any[]) {
        // stores modifiers on the node...
        return this;
      }
    };
  };

  return factory as unknown as (props: P) => any;
}
Usage
